# /usr/lib/tmpfiles.d/weftdb.conf — the runtime directory a host install of WeftDB expects.
#
# Part of PAC-11 (1.0 plan): the systemd unit, the user and the env file. `systemd-tmpfiles
# --create` reads this at boot (and `systemd-tmpfiles-setup.service` runs it for you), and
# `RuntimeDirectory=` in the unit creates `/run/weftdb` as well, so the two mechanisms must
# agree — which raises the obvious question of why this file exists at all.
#
# # Why both
#
# `RuntimeDirectory=weftdb` in the unit creates the directory *when the service starts*, owned by
# the service user, mode 0755 minus the umask — and removes it when the service stops. That is
# right for scratch state and wrong for a keys file: an operator who puts `/run/weftdb/api.keys`
# in place before first boot has the directory re-created under the service, and an operator who
# starts the server outside systemd (`weft-server serve` by hand, which is a normal thing to do
# while debugging) gets no directory at all and a confusing "No such file or directory (os error
# 2)" naming the keys path.
#
# So the unit does not set `RuntimeDirectory=` — it is left to this file, which creates the
# directory at boot with an explicit mode and owner, and leaves it alone when the service stops.
# The cost is that nothing removes stale files here automatically; `/run` is a tmpfs and is gone
# at the next boot, which is the same lifetime, and a keys file that should survive a reboot
# belongs in `/etc/weftdb` anyway.
#
# # Fields
#
# `d path mode uid gid age argument` — an age of `-` means "never clean up".
#
# The mode is 0750 rather than the 0755 `RuntimeDirectory=` would have given: this is where the
# keys file goes by default, and the directory holding it should not be world-listable. The
# group is the service group so an operator can add a keys file as a member of `weftdb` without
# going through root.
d /run/weftdb 0750 weftdb weftdb -

# The parent of the state directory is `/var/lib`, which the distribution owns and which needs
# nothing from us; only `/var/lib/weftdb` itself is ours, and the sysusers file creates it. This
# file carries no `d /var/lib/weftdb` line on purpose — two files creating the same path with two
# different modes is a race that `systemd-tmpfiles` resolves by taking the last line it reads,
# and the sysusers copy is the one the installer documents.
